[size=200][b]UTTchain[/b][/size]
[size=120]Unified Trading Terminal • Robinhood Chain Protocol Layer[/size][/center]

[b]UTTchain[/b] is a protocol and infrastructure project being developed for the [b]Unified Trading Terminal (UTT)[/b] and [b]Unified Trading Terminal Token (UTTT)[/b], built around Robinhood Chain.

The project is being designed [b]onchain-first[/b]. Robinhood Chain should carry deterministic protocol logic, identity, authorization, settlement, and publicly verifiable state wherever EVM execution is technically suitable and economically reasonable.

[b]Server-independent launch, optional services later[/b]

UTTchain production launch and core protocol use must [b]not[/b] require users to run a second always-on local server, daemon, companion machine, or project-operated application server.

The architecture priority is:

[list=1]
[*][b]Robinhood Chain smart contracts and chain state[/b]
[*][b]Browser-side execution and wallet interaction[/b]
[*][b]Replaceable RPC, oracle, and indexer infrastructure[/b]
[*][b]Optional hosted/serverless services where useful or technically necessary[/b]
[/list]

The server-independent launch rule does not prohibit adding a server later. Post-launch hosted services may provide indexing, performance, private application state, notifications, CEX connectivity, richer integrations, or other extensions. They should remain additive and replaceable rather than silently becoming the authority for canonical on-chain state or a mandatory dependency for core protocol functions.

Existing local UTT may remain an independent application and integration surface, but it will not be a required runtime dependency for UTTchain's web/onchain protocol functions.

[b]Extensibility and smart-contract upgradeability[/b]

Future server-backed features do not require every launch contract to be proxy-upgradeable.

UTTchain will prefer:

[list=1]
[*]new browser/server capabilities that do not alter canonical contracts;
[*]new versioned or modular contracts when on-chain functionality must evolve;
[*]explicit migration/supersession where immutable contracts can be replaced safely;
[*]proxy-based upgrades only where a specific component demonstrates a real requirement.
[/list]

Any contract upgrade authority must be explicit, bounded, reviewed, and tested. Upgradeable components require storage-layout review, authority/timelock analysis, upgrade-path tests, deployment reconciliation, and documented recovery/migration behavior. Critical token or supply authority will not become upgradeable merely for convenience.

Smart contracts cannot directly run a browser interface, securely retain centralized-exchange API secrets, or make arbitrary authenticated HTTP requests to external exchanges. Where a workload cannot safely or technically execute inside the EVM, UTTchain will prefer browser execution, cryptographically verifiable data delivery, replaceable public infrastructure, or optional hosted/serverless extensions rather than requiring localhost software from the user.

